      "description": "Chicago Parking Meters operates and maintains the City of Chicago's on-street metered parking system under a long-dated public-private concession. Its customer base is the daily parking demand of residents, commuters, businesses, and visitors using approximately 36,000 on-street parking spaces across Chicago. The business follows a concession-based infrastructure model in which revenues are tied to meter collections while operating obligations include maintenance, payment systems, and contractual compliance with the city. Public sources describe it as the third-largest metered parking system in the United States and one of the first large-scale U.S. parking-meter concessions. The concession closed in 2009 for 75 years and the system has since been modernized with pay boxes, mobile payments, and ongoing operating and maintenance requirements. Current public materials identify Morgan Stanley Infrastructure Partners, Allianz Capital Partners, and ADIA as owners, although current ownership percentages are not publicly disclosed in primary public sources.",
